# Byte-compiled / optimized / DLL files
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class

# Rust
/target/
**/*.rs.bk
*.pdb
Cargo.lock.bak

# WebAssembly artifacts
*.wasm
*.cwasm
!tests/**/*.wasm
!fixtures/**/*.wasm

# Node
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# Build outputs
/dist/
/build/
/out/

# Environment — never commit secrets
#
# `.env.*` covers EVERY variant. The narrow rules that used to be here
# (`.env.local`, `.env.*.local`) left `.env.production`, `.env.bak` and every other
# copy **unignored** — and copying a `.env` before editing it is a normal thing to
# do, so the gap was reachable by accident rather than by carelessness. `docs/.env`'s
# own header asserted a `.env.*` rule existed; it did not. See `§O-267`.
#
# `.env.example` is the single exception and IS tracked: it is the template that
# makes the credential set discoverable without the credentials. `docs/.env.example`
# is referenced by `.coderabbit.yaml` and `docs/AGENT-HANDBOOK.md`; this rule is what
# makes those references true.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.key
*.pem
!tests/**/fixtures/**/*.pem

# Editors
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json
!.vscode/settings.shared.json
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# Scratch / temporary verification crates
.scratch/

# Local review/feedback documents kept in the working tree but not tracked.
#
# docs/Windows-Linux-Docker.md is a review conversation captured from a chat
# session. It is useful locally and it is not a specification document, so it
# stays on disk and out of the repository -- the same treatment .scratch/ gets.
docs/Windows-Linux-Docker.md

# The agent handbook: the working memory of the AI agents who built this
# repository -- the process, the tooling, the traps, the lessons.
#
# It is ignored rather than committed for the same reason .scratch/ is: it
# describes how the work is done, not what the product is. Committing it would
# put a document about the *process* into the product's public surface, where a
# reader looking for the specification would find a guide to CI instead. The
# project's specification (QQQ-Proposal-V1.md), ledger (QQQ-Checklist-V1.md) and
# decision record (QQQ-Observations-and-Memories.md) are tracked and are the
# public account of the work; this file is the private one.
docs/AGENT-HANDBOOK.md

# Fuzzing — `SEC-012`/`SEC-013`.
#
# `fuzz/` is a SEPARATE cargo workspace, so the root `/target/` rule above (which
# is anchored) does not cover it. Without these lines, `git add -A` in the fuzz
# directory would stage several gigabytes of build output — which is both a
# repository-size problem and, worse, a source of *stale artifacts committed as
# truth*, the failure `§O-072` records from the other direction.
fuzz/target/

# The reference application's build output — `SRV-018`.
#
# `examples/orders-api` is a **guest** crate, so it is not a member of the host
# workspace and the anchored `/target/` rule above does not reach it. The same
# reasoning as `fuzz/target/`: `git add -A` would stage hundreds of megabytes of
# build output, and — worse than the size — a committed `target/` is
# **stale artifacts stored as truth**, which is the failure `§O-072` records.
#
# Note that `examples/orders-api/Cargo.lock` IS committed. That is deliberate and
# different: a lockfile is a resolved dependency decision, which is exactly what a
# reference app should pin, while `target/` is the output of applying it.
examples/orders-api/target/

# The mutating corpus harness's exclusive lock (`tools/self_test_xrefs.py`).
#
# Ignored because it is a **transient claim on a running process**, not project
# content: it is created at the start of `main()`, removed on every exit path, and it
# names a PID that is meaningless in any other checkout. A `SIGKILL` or a container
# stop can leave it behind, and the file is designed for exactly that — the next run
# reads the PID, finds it gone, and reclaims the lock with a message.
#
# Committing it would be the `target/` mistake in miniature: a stale artifact stored as
# truth, and one that would make the harness refuse to run for whoever pulled it. See
# `§O-193` for the two overlapping mutators that made the lock necessary.
.self_test_xrefs.lock

# Crash artifacts. These are deliberately NOT committed: libFuzzer names them
# `crash-<hash>` and their content is the bug, so the correct destination is a
# **corpus entry** in `crates/*/tests/fuzz_corpus.rs` — which is the promotion
# step that turns a one-off crash into a test that runs on every commit. An
# artifact left in the tree is a bug nobody re-runs.
fuzz/artifacts/

# libFuzzer's evolving corpus. It is machine-generated and grows without bound;
# the *curated* seed corpus in `fuzz/corpus/` IS committed, because a seed corpus
# that is not in the repository is a seed corpus every contributor regenerates
# from nothing.
fuzz/corpus/*/
!fuzz/corpus/README.md

# Local tooling
.tools/
*.log

# Graf's local code-graph index (`.graf/index.db`). It is a per-machine SQLite
# cache rebuilt from the tree, so it is a build artifact rather than source: it
# is tens of megabytes, and a committed copy goes stale the moment a file moves.
# Graf's own usage guide says to keep it out of version control.
.graf/

# SBOMs are generated per-crate by `cargo cyclonedx` and uploaded as a CI artifact
# (`SEC-028`), not committed. A committed SBOM is wrong the moment a dependency
# moves, and a stale supply-chain document is worse than none: it answers the
# question "am I affected?" with the state of the tree at some earlier commit.
# The *artifacts* from a release build are the published SBOM, and they carry that
# release's commit.
*.cdx.json
/sbom/

docs/TEMP-HANDOFF.md
